>
  <p class="lede">
    I build the connective tissue between product, engineering and marketing —
    and I've worked from inside each of them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Over fifteen years I've grown from senior software engineer to technical
    lead to product owner to senior product leadership. Today I'm the first
    product manager at an AI-first company building products for financial
    advice and insurance, brought in to establish the product management
    function: strategy and roadmap, outcome metrics, an AI-driven delivery
    lifecycle and a regular customer validation loop.
  </p>

  <p>
    Before that I was Head of Product Marketing and Principal Product Manager
    at an Australian-owned global product company in the
    Data&nbsp;Risk&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Compliance space, leading the product
    marketing team and partnering with regional sales and executives to shape
    and execute go-to-market strategy.
  </p>

  <h2>Career at a glance</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Now</strong> · First Product Manager, AI products for financial advice and insurance (since Aug&nbsp;2026)</li>
    <li><strong>Jan–Aug 2026</strong> · Head of Product Marketing and Principal Product Manager, enterprise Data Risk &amp; Compliance</li>
    <li><strong>Earlier</strong> · Senior Product Manager, Product Owner, Technical Lead, Senior Software Engineer</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Because I've sat in the engineering chair, the product-owner chair and the
    marketing chair, I can translate an ambiguous, subjective requirement into
    something scoped, estimable, priced and shippable — and then stand behind
    the outcome. I gathered Data Governance needs from many enterprise
    customers, harmonised them into a coherent feature set, and used C4-model
    analysis to drive effort estimation, work breakdown and pricing. I designed
    the <strong>GTM Factory</strong>, a repeatable product-marketing framework
    that compresses time-to-market by running a virtual team deep into
    engineering.
  </p>

  <h2>Applied AI, hands-on</h2>
  <p>
    I run an AI-Driven Development Lifecycle with feature-flagged, progressive
    rollouts, build Claude-based compliance review tools, and lead AI workflow
    and acceptance testing on de-identified client data. Earlier I prototyped
    an AI Asset Description generator and an AI Code Explainer, and I design
    AI-driven automation across product and marketing workflows. I work with GenAI models, RAG, prompt engineering, MCP
    and AI agents (Claude, n8n, Zapier), and recently completed an intensive
    six-month GenAI program at IIT Patna.
  </p>

  <h2>Why education keeps pulling me back</h2>
  <p>
    The thread runs through everything I've done outside work: a Masters in
    Public Administration, editing a student e-journal, coordinating campus
    placements, and volunteering to teach underprivileged children through
    Project Manthan. I'm drawn to the education sector because it's where
    product craft, technology and human outcomes meet most directly — and it's
    one of the places I most want to apply what I build. The EdTech case
    studies on this site are explorations of exactly that.
  </p>

  <h2>How I think about the work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Point of view first.</strong> Strategy is a stance, not a status report.</li>
    <li><strong>Discovery to production.</strong> Own the outcome, not the handoff.</li>
    <li><strong>Bias to iterate.</strong> Start small, ship, learn — "Say NO! to Waterfall".</li>
    <li><strong>Bridge the silos.</strong> The best leverage lives between product, engineering and marketing.</li>
  </ul>

</ProseLayout>
